  "situation": "The council is considering whether to make a major resource shift toward limited-war capability or retain a balanced military program. The Joint Chiefs cautioned that limited-war studies were not a valid basis for programming or decision-making, and assessed that any one limited-war situation could require prompt mobilization and other measures. They estimated that airlift would be short during the first twenty days of a Korea-scale resumption of hostilities, while sealift was generally adequate with a possible shortage during the first sixty days. Military logistics guidance had been issued but was not fully implemented, and the services had differing readiness and equipment concerns. Southeast Asia operations faced logistical limitations, with some countermeasures already in place or under consideration. Additional funding could alleviate many deficiencies, but not all could be remedied at once; the planning constraint was to avoid an unacceptable degradation of general-war capability for contingencies within the general magnitude of the Korean contingency plan.",
